kubectl apply

kubectl edit opens a live Kubernetes resource directly in your terminal's default text editor (like vi or nano), allowing you to modify its configuration and immediately apply the changes to the cluster as soon as you save and exit.


How kubectl edit Works

• Live cluster modification. Unlike kubectl apply (which reads from your local YAML file), 

kubectl edit pod my-nginx-pod ; fetches the live object directly from the Kubernetes API server.

• Instant save and patch. When you save and close the editor, Kubernetes validates your changes and updates the live object in place.

• Same Pod immutability rules apply. Just like kubectl apply, if you run kubectl edit pod and try to change an immutable field (like containerPort or env), Kubernetes will reject the save and reopen the editor with an error message at the top explaining which field is forbidden.


Important Warning About Drift

• Local files go out of sync. If you fix a wrong image name using kubectl edit pod my-nginx-pod, your pod will start running, but your local pod.yaml file will still have the old, broken image name.

• Future overwrites. If you later run kubectl apply -f pod.yaml from your local file, Kubernetes will overwrite your live kubectl edit fix with the old broken configuration from your file.